  // ── Page Integrity (9th type) ──
  'page.iv1m': '1 min', 'page.iv5m': '5 min', 'page.iv10m': '10 min', 'page.iv15m': '15 min',
  'page.iv30m': '30 min', 'page.iv1h': '1 hr', 'page.iv12h': '12 hr', 'page.iv24h': '24 hr',
  'page.title':          'Page Integrity Monitoring',
  'page.subtitle':       'Verifies the page loads healthily at the code level: broken links/resources, mixed content and content anomalies.',
  'page.howTitle': 'How and where are these checks done?',
  'page.how1': 'HTML fetch + parse: the page HTML is downloaded (through the corporate proxy, over 443) and jsoup extracts the resource inventory — img (src/srcset), CSS (link[stylesheet]), JS (script[src]), in/out links (a[href]), iframes, favicon and preload fonts. Relative URLs are resolved against the page URL; deduplicated.',
  'page.how2': 'Resource verification: each resource is checked with bounded concurrency (politeness + single-pod load guard) — HEAD first, falling back to GET if the server does not support it (405/501). 404/410 and 5xx = BROKEN, over the threshold = SLOW, no response at all (connection timeout) = TIMEOUT. One request per resource, identifiable User-Agent (SiteMonitor-PageCheck).',
  'page.how2b': 'Timeouts: resources unreachable from the monitoring server\'s network vantage point (especially third-party CDN/analytics) may load fine in a real browser. Timeouts have their OWN counter — they never mix into the BROKEN counter (a definitively broken URL and a non-responding URL are reported separately). The per-monitor "watch timeouts" toggle (on by default) only affects status/alerting: when off, timeouts don\'t flag the page and raise no alarm; they still show in the counter and the issues table.',
  'page.how3': 'Mixed content: a resource loaded over http:// on an HTTPS page is flagged as "mixed content" — browsers may block/warn on these. There is a per-monitor "alert on mixed content" toggle (on by default); when off, mixed content is still detected and shown in the table but does not raise an alarm.',
  'page.how4': 'First / third-party split: if the resource host is the same origin as the page (same domain/subdomain) it is first-party, otherwise third-party (·3P badge). By default only first-party breakage sends an email alarm (third-party ad/analytics resources break often and are outside the site owner\'s control); "also include third-party" can be enabled per monitor.',
  'page.how5': 'Two modes: Single Page checks only the target URL frequently (minutes). Site Crawl walks same-origin links within a configured depth + max-page limit INFREQUENTLY (daily) — it honors robots.txt Disallow rules, seeds from sitemap.xml if present, and crawls one site at a time. "Exclude patterns" filter out intentional 404s / external tracking pixels.',
  'page.how6': 'Status & alarm: OK (clean) → DEGRADED (page loads but has broken/mixed resources) → DOWN (main page cannot be fetched at all). An alarm fires only when a problem PERSISTS across N consecutive checks (one-off hiccups don\'t produce false positives) and auto-closes once resolved. DOWN → CRITICAL, DEGRADED → HIGH. False-positive guard: inconclusive statuses (403/429/503 WAF/rate-limit — shown as "Inconclusive") do NOT alarm. External links (a[href]) only count on DEFINITIVE death: 404/410 or the target being entirely unresolvable/unconnectable (e.g. a site whose DNS record was removed); external-link 5xx/timeout/inconclusive still never alarms — table-only.',
  'page.how7': 'Security & retention: EVERY requested URL — the main page, each parsed resource and each redirect hop — passes an SSRF check; resources pointing at internal-network / cloud-metadata addresses are blocked (URLs from parsing are treated as untrusted as user input). A content hash + body-size delta are recorded as a defacement signal (info-only for now). Only PROBLEM resources are stored; history is kept for a configurable period and purged nightly.',
  'page.how8': 'When you see BROKEN: "Broken" = the page loads but that piece inside it does not (NOT an outage). Check the HTTP code — 404/410: file gone/moved → update/remove the reference on the page or restore the file (invalidate caches if the name changed after a deploy); 5xx: the resource\'s server/CDN is failing → escalate to that service\'s owner; Timeout/no connection → have network-firewall access and the resource server checked. Priority: broken CSS/JS breaks the page (high), a broken image is cosmetic, a broken internal link sends users to a 404. The alarm closes automatically once the resource is fixed.',
